**New Evidence**
- Scholz’s labor ministry framed Bürgergeld as a dignity- and opportunity-oriented reform: more security, more respect, less bureaucracy, and stronger qualification/continuing-education support. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Das neue Bürgergeld: Mehr Respekt und Sicherheit, weniger Bürokratie","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/das-neue-buergergeld-mehr-respekt-und-sicherheit-weniger-buerokratie.html","claim":"The new Bürgergeld was presented as more security, more respect, and more freedom, with help made more citizen-friendly, less bureaucratic, and more targeted, and with qualification and continuing education emphasized as the path back into work.","locator":"lines 794-798","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry announcement of a flagship 2022 social reform under the Scholz cabinet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period intent and design.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet policy under Scholz via BMAS; it is institutional policy rather than a personal order, but clearly attributable to his government.","contrary_evidence":["This is a stated reform design, not evidence of delivered outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- The Bürgergeld law was enacted in 2022, published on 20 December 2022, and entered into force on 1 January 2023, updating the working-age basic-security system. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bürgergeld-Gesetz","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Gesetze-und-Gesetzesvorhaben/buergergeld-gesetz.html","claim":"The Bürgergeld law was published on 20 December 2022 and entered into force on 1 January 2023, with the ministry describing it as bringing the basic-security system for jobseekers up to date.","locator":"lines 792-803, 818-820","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry law page with enactment status and implementation timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 enactment for a core social-reform package.","ruler_attribution":"Coalition legislation under Scholz; implementation was shared with parliament, Bundesrat, and jobcenters.","contrary_evidence":["Most implementation began in 2023, so the 2022 record is stronger on approval than delivery."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The government suspended sanctions in basic security for one year starting 1 July 2022, as a bridge to the later Bürgergeld reform. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gesetz zur Regelung eines Sanktionsmoratoriums in der Grundsicherung für Arbeitsuchende","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Gesetze-und-Gesetzesvorhaben/gesetz-zur-regelung-eines-sanktionsmoratoriums.html","claim":"The 11th SGB II amendment, the so-called sanctions moratorium, entered into force on 1 July 2022 and suspended sanctions in basic security for jobseekers for one year, as an interim step before the Bürgergeld redesign.","locator":"lines 794-796","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry law page with explicit date and policy effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Coalition policy under Scholz, implemented through BMAS and the legislative process.","contrary_evidence":["Some reporting conditions still allowed penalties for repeated missed appointments."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The Bundestag debate on Bürgergeld presented the reform as reliable protection for people in existential need and as a response to the pandemic and low-wage insecurity. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bürgergeld im Bundestag beraten","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/buergergeld-bundestag.html","claim":"The ministry said people in existential need should be reliably protected, and that the reform responded to the pandemic-era experience of soloself-employed workers and low-wage employees needing supplementary basic security.","locator":"lines 777-777","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry page summarizing the Bundestag debate and the reform rationale, but more rhetorical than operational.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 legislative process evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Scholz government through BMAS; this captures stated policy logic rather than delivery by a single officeholder.","contrary_evidence":["It is legislative intent, not proof of implementation quality."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.5","6B.7","6B.8"]}

- The minimum wage rose to 12 euros per hour from 1 October 2022, with more than six million people benefiting, especially women and people in eastern Germany. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Der Mindestlohn steigt auf 12 Euro","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/mindestlohn-steigt-auf-12-euro.html","claim":"The Bundestag approved the government bill to raise the statutory minimum wage to 12 euros per hour from 1 October 2022, benefiting over six million people, especially women and workers in eastern Germany.","locator":"lines 794-798","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry release on a passed federal bill with specific beneficiary scale and timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 wage and household-income policy.","ruler_attribution":"Coalition policy under Scholz and the Bundestag; not a personal administrative act, but clearly a government choice.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation still eroded real purchasing power in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Wohngeld Plus was approved in 2022 as the largest housing-benefit reform since the program began, to support 4.5 million people in two million households, including many single parents, low-income workers, pensioners, and care-home residents. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Geld für die Wohnkosten im Winter","publisher":"BMWSB","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.bmwsb.bund.de/SharedDocs/pressemitteilungen/DE/2022/09/wohngeld-plus.html","claim":"Wohngeld Plus was described as the largest housing-benefit reform since the program began and was projected to support 4.5 million people in two million households, including many single parents, low-income workers, pensioners, and care-home residents.","locator":"lines 279-283","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release with concrete scale and beneficiary groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy decision with 2023 implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet policy under Scholz; implementation is shared with the Länder and local housing offices.","contrary_evidence":["The main cash effect begins in 2023, so this is mostly design/approval evidence."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Germany moved Ukrainian refugees into basic social-security coverage from 1 June 2022, via jobcenters, with access to integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health insurance. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Couverture de base pour les réfugiés ukrainiens","publisher":"Gouvernement fédéral","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-fr/service/archives/couverture-de-base-ukrainiens-2028896","claim":"Registered Ukrainian refugees were to receive extended basic social-security support from 1 June 2022, with jobcenter access, integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health coverage.","locator":"lines 164-171","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal-government archive page with explicit cabinet decision and service details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 refugee-protection and welfare policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal cabinet action under Scholz; delivery shared with Länder and local jobcenters.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy-design document, not a full outcomes evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Scholz’s government publicly committed to support Ukrainian refugees with housing, medical care, schooling, and a secure future, plus substantial humanitarian aid. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rede von Bundeskanzler Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-de/service/newsletter-und-abos/bulletin/rede-von-bundeskanzler-olaf-scholz-2025152","claim":"Scholz said Ukrainian refugees were welcome and that the government would ensure accommodation, medical care, school instruction, and a secure future, alongside additional humanitarian funding.","locator":"lines 195-197","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary chancellor speech with direct welfare and refugee-protection commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period statement.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Scholz as chancellor.","contrary_evidence":["This is a commitment speech, not a delivery audit."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The April 2022 supplementary budget explicitly aimed to relieve citizens and soften the effects of Russia’s war on Ukraine, including 2 billion euros for refugees, 1.45 billion euros for humanitarian aid, a 300 euro energy allowance, a 100 euro child bonus, a 100 euro extra social-benefits payment, and a 9-euro ticket. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Der Ergänzungshaushalt: gezielt Bürgerinnen und Bürger entlasten","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/05/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/2b-ergaenzungshaushalt.html","claim":"The supplementary budget was adopted to relieve citizens and mitigate the consequences of Russia's war on Ukraine, including 2 billion euros for refugees, 1.45 billion euros for humanitarian aid, a 300 euro energy allowance, a 100 euro child bonus, an extra 100 euro social-benefits payment, and a 9-euro public-transport ticket.","locator":"lines 138-160","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary finance-ministry budget note with specific amounts and beneficiary measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-response fiscal policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal-government action under Scholz; it is a cabinet and budget decision, not a personal program.","contrary_evidence":["The same source shows the state was responding to a severe shock rather than delivering a stable baseline."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The October 2022 energy shield was a 200 billion euro package, announced by Scholz, Lindner, and Habeck, intended to protect households and firms, maintain purchasing power, and preserve energy-saving incentives through gas and heating price brakes. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Wirtschaftlicher Abwehrschirm und Gaspreisbremse: mit Entschlossenheit durch die Energiekrise","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-10-01","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/10/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/2b-mit-entschlossenheit-durch-die-energiekrise.html","claim":"The government announced a 200 billion euro economic shield, presented by Scholz, Lindner, and Habeck, to protect households and firms, preserve purchasing power, and maintain energy-saving incentives through gas and heating price brakes.","locator":"lines 139-147, 183-184","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary finance-ministry policy note with named leadership, package size, and design logic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-response policy.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level crisis management under Scholz; collective cabinet action rather than personal administrative control.","contrary_evidence":["The package is costly and some benefits were forward-looking rather than immediate in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The IMF said Germany expanded support for vulnerable households, cut fuel taxes, and provided liquidity support to firms, and judged the response to the Ukraine spillovers timely and well designed. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Germany","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/07/19/pr22268-germany-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-germany","claim":"The IMF said Germany was expanding income support for vulnerable households, cutting fuel taxes, and providing liquidity support to firms, and commended the authorities' timely and well-designed response to the Ukraine spillovers.","locator":"lines 243-249","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with concrete policy content and evaluative judgment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy assessment during the shock year.","ruler_attribution":"External appraisal of the Scholz government's response; not a personal judgment on every implementing agency.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also noted the inflation and gas-shock environment constrained outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The OECD said the government acted swiftly to support households and firms and secure energy supply, but also said the support was not well targeted to vulnerable households and highly exposed firms. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Germany 2023","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-germany-2023_9642a3f5-en/full-report/component-4.html","claim":"The OECD said the government acted swiftly to support households and firms and secure energy supply, while noting that some support measures were not well targeted to vulnerable households and highly exposed firms.","locator":"lines 3928-3944","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral economic assessment with detailed package description and a clear critique of targeting quality.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retroactive assessment of the 2022 response and immediate design choices.","ruler_attribution":"External evaluation of the Scholz government's policy package, not a direct measure of personal ministerial management.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD explicitly criticized targeting quality."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Destatis reported annual inflation of 7.9 percent in 2022, driven mainly by energy and food price shocks after the war in Ukraine, while relief measures partly mitigated the increase. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation rate at +7.9% in 2022","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_022_611.html","claim":"Germany's consumer prices rose 7.9 percent in 2022, mainly due to energy and food price spikes after the war in Ukraine, and relief measures partly mitigated the increase.","locator":"lines 117-128","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics with exact annual figures and explanatory context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published immediately after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz's government rather than a clean ruler-caused effect; useful as a constraint on welfare performance.","contrary_evidence":["The same release shows households still faced very large price increases despite mitigation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said real earnings fell 4.0 percent in 2022, the largest loss since 2008. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nach Revision: Reallöhne im Jahr 2022 um 4,0 % gegenüber 2021 gesunken","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-04-27","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2023/04/PD23_166_62321.html","claim":"Real earnings in Germany decreased by an average 4.0 percent in 2022 compared with 2021, the largest loss since 2008.","locator":"lines 131-138","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with revised annual estimate and clear year-on-year result.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data, albeit published with a later revision.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; the loss reflects the inflation shock as much as policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["This is negative welfare evidence and shows that price shocks overwhelmed nominal gains."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said 20.9 percent of Germany’s population, about 17.3 million people, were at risk of poverty or social exclusion in 2022, almost unchanged from 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Just over one-fifth of Germany’s population at risk of poverty or social exclusion","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-05-16","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/05/PE23_190_63.html","claim":"Just over 17.3 million people in Germany were affected by poverty or social exclusion in 2022, equal to 20.9 percent of the population, and the value was almost unchanged from 2021.","locator":"lines 113-113","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with explicit annual poverty-risk figures and year-on-year comparison.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; it constrains claims of broad human-development improvement.","contrary_evidence":["The indicator did not materially improve year on year."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said employment in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, the highest level since German reunification, up by 589,000 from 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Employment in 2022 at highest level since German unification","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-02","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_001_13321.html","claim":"Average employment in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, the highest level since German reunification, rising by 589,000 from 2021.","locator":"lines 112-116","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with a clear headline figure and year-on-year change.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 labor-market outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome during Scholz’s first full year, but partly inherited and affected by migration and preexisting labor-market strength.","contrary_evidence":["Employment strength did not offset the simultaneous erosion in real earnings and persistent poverty risk."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 25 to 40 documents across BMAS, BMWSB, BMF, Bundesregierung, IMF, OECD, and Destatis material.
- I kept the strongest non-duplicative items and rejected later follow-up pages from 2023-2026 that only repeated the same programs or moved outside the 2022 target period.
- I dropped generic landing pages, navigation-heavy pages, and duplicate language mirrors unless they contained a distinct target-period claim with a usable locator.
- I also avoided overusing the same fiscal package across multiple publishers when it did not add a materially new claim.
- The main rejection pattern was duplication, not lack of evidence: Bürgergeld, Wohngeld Plus, refugee support, energy relief, and 2022 macro outcomes each had multiple overlapping pages, so I retained only the clearest instances.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on 6B.4 is still thinner than the policy-output evidence. The dossier shows strong welfare-oriented choices, but not much direct inspection of staffing quality, administrative capacity, or program execution inside federal and local agencies.
- 6B.9 is only partly evidenced. Bürgergeld and Wohngeld Plus point toward institutional durability, but the 2022 record is stronger on design and adoption than on long-run durability.
- Distributional coverage is still incomplete for disabled people and some minority subgroups.
- More direct implementation evidence would help for the social-service system and for whether 2022 reforms actually improved access by year-end rather than mainly setting up 2023 effects.
- If you want, I can do one more pass focused only on 6B.4, 6B.5, and 6B.9.
